Great topic—Cluster Autoscaler (CA) is what makes your Kubernetes cluster scale nodes automatically based on workload demand. I’ll explain:
What it is & how it works internally
How it decides to scale up/down
End-to-end setup on AWS (EKS / EC2 ASG)
Common pitfalls & debugging

1. What is Cluster Autoscaler?
Cluster Autoscaler is a component that:
Automatically adjusts the number of nodes (EC2 instances) in your cluster
Simple idea
Pods increase → Need more nodes → CA adds nodes
Pods decrease → Nodes idle → CA removes nodes

2. Where does it run?
It runs as a Pod inside your cluster (usually in kube-system)

3. Core concept (VERY IMPORTANT)
Cluster Autoscaler reacts to:
UNSCHEDULABLE Pods
What is unschedulable?
A Pod that cannot be placed on any node due to:
Not enough CPU
Not enough memory
Node selectors mismatch
Taints/tolerations

4. Scale UP flow
1. Pod created
2. Scheduler tries to place it
3. No node has enough resources ❌
4. Pod becomes "Pending"
5. Cluster Autoscaler detects it
6. CA increases node group size (ASG)
7. New EC2 node is created
8. Pod gets scheduled ✔

5. Scale DOWN flow
1. Some nodes are underutilized
2. CA checks if Pods can be moved elsewhere
3. If safe:
   → drains node
   → deletes EC2 instance

6. Important AWS concept
Cluster Autoscaler works with:
Auto Scaling Groups (ASG)
Mapping
Kubernetes Node Group → AWS Auto Scaling Group

7. Requirements for AWS setup
Before installing CA, you must have:
1. EKS Cluster
Created using:
eksctl OR
Terraform OR
AWS Console
2. Node Group (ASG)
Must have:
min size
max size
desired size
3. Tags on ASG (VERY IMPORTANT)
k8s.io/cluster-autoscaler/enabled = true
k8s.io/cluster-autoscaler/<cluster-name> = owned
Without these → CA will NOT work

8. IAM Role (critical step)
Cluster Autoscaler needs permission to:
Scale ASG
Describe instances
IAM Policy
Attach policy like:

{
  "Effect": "Allow",
  "Action": [
    "autoscaling:DescribeAutoScalingGroups",
    "autoscaling:SetDesiredCapacity",
    "autoscaling:TerminateInstanceInAutoScalingGroup",
    "ec2:DescribeLaunchTemplateVersions"
  ],
  "Resource": "*"
}
Best practice
Use IAM Role for Service Account (IRSA)

9. Create ServiceAccount with IAM role
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: cluster-autoscaler
  namespace: kube-system
  annotations:
    eks.amazonaws.com/role-arn: <IAM_ROLE_ARN>

10. Deploy Cluster Autoscaler
Official Deployment (important parts)
containers:
- image: registry.k8s.io/autoscaling/cluster-autoscaler:v1.xx
  name: cluster-autoscaler
Key arguments
command:
- ./cluster-autoscaler
- --cloud-provider=aws
- --cluster-name=<your-cluster-name>
- --balance-similar-node-groups
- --skip-nodes-with-system-pods=false
- --skip-nodes-with-local-storage=false
Auto-discovery of ASG
- --node-group-auto-discovery=asg:tag=k8s.io/cluster-autoscaler/enabled,k8s.io/cluster-autoscaler/<cluster-name>
This tells CA:
“Find ASGs using these tags”

11. Apply deployment
kubectl apply -f cluster-autoscaler.yaml

12. Verify
kubectl get pods -n kube-system
Look for:
cluster-autoscaler-xxxxx

13. Test scaling (very important)
Step 1: Create heavy workload
resources:
  requests:
    cpu: 2000m
    memory: 2Gi
Step 2: Observe
kubectl get pods
Pods go into:
Pending
Step 3: Watch nodes
kubectl get nodes -w
New node appears ✔

14. Scale down test
Delete workload
Wait (default ~10 mins)
Node gets terminated ✔

15. Important parameters (must know)
--scale-down-delay-after-add
Wait before scaling down

--scale-down-unneeded-time
How long node must be idle
--max-nodes-total
Upper limit

16. Common issues
CA not scaling
Check:

ASG tags missing 
IAM permissions missing 
Wrong cluster name 
Pod not triggering scale
Check:
Requests defined? (very important)
Scheduler constraints?
NodeSelector issues?
Nodes not scaling down
Check:
Pods with local storage
System pods
PDB (PodDisruptionBudget)

17. Real-world use case
Scenario
Daytime → high traffic → more Pods → CA adds nodes
Night → low traffic → fewer Pods → CA removes nodes
Benefit
Save cost + automatic scaling

18. One-line memory
Cluster Autoscaler = scales NODES based on pending Pods

Final summary
Watches unschedulable Pods
Talks to AWS ASG
Adds/removes EC2 nodes automatically
Runs inside cluster



