explaination:


You’ve put together a complete picture: resource requests/limits + Cluster Autoscaler setup. 
The confusion you mentioned (“no new nodes even after increasing resources”) is actually the key concept here. Let’s go step by step and connect everything clearly.

1. First: Requests vs Limits (VERY IMPORTANT for autoscaling)
From your Deployment:
resources:
  requests:
    memory: "512Mi"
    cpu: "1"
  limits:
    memory: "1Gi"
    cpu: "1"
What Kubernetes uses for scheduling?
ONLY requests are used for scheduling
Scheduler decision = based on requests
NOT limits
Meaning
requests → minimum guaranteed resources
limits → max container can use
Why autoscaler didn’t trigger?
You wrote:
“increase the resources and no new nodes are coming”
Possible reasons:
Case 1: Requests still fit in existing node
Example:
Node capacity = 4 CPU
Your pod request = 1 CPU
2 replicas → total = 2 CPU
Still fits → NO scaling
Case 2: Only limits increased
limits:
  cpu: 4
But requests still:
requests:
  cpu: 1
Scheduler sees only 1 CPU → no scaling
Correct way to trigger autoscaler
Increase requests, not just limits
Example:
requests:
  cpu: 3
  memory: 2Gi
Now:
Pod cannot fit → becomes Pending → Autoscaler triggers

2. Cluster Autoscaler concept (connected to your example)
If Pod = Pending (unschedulable)
→ Cluster Autoscaler adds node
Full flow
1. Pod created
2. Scheduler tries to place it
3. Not enough resources ❌
4. Pod = Pending
5. Autoscaler detects it
6. ASG scaled up
7. New node created
8. Pod scheduled ✔

3. Your AWS Setup Explained
Step 1: IAM Policy
You created:
autoscaling:SetDesiredCapacity
autoscaling:TerminateInstanceInAutoScalingGroup
Meaning:
“Allow autoscaler to increase/decrease EC2 instances”
Why needed?
Cluster Autoscaler must:
Increase node count
Terminate nodes
Step 2: Attach policy to EKS role
You attached policy to:
EKS Cluster IAM Role
Better practice:
Use IRSA (IAM Role for ServiceAccount)
But your method still works for learning

4. Step 3: Deploy Cluster Autoscaler
ServiceAccount
name: cluster-autoscaler
namespace: kube-system
Identity for autoscaler pod
ClusterRole
This big block:
resources: ["pods", "nodes", "pv", "pvc", ...]
Autoscaler needs to:
Watch pods
Check node usage
Evict pods
Read cluster state
ClusterRoleBinding
subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
Connects:
ServiceAccount → Permissions
Deployment (MAIN PART)
Image
image: registry.k8s.io/autoscaling/cluster-autoscaler:v1.26.2
Actual autoscaler software
Important arguments
--cloud-provider=aws
Tells autoscaler:
Use AWS APIs
--node-group-auto-discovery
asg:tag=k8s.io/cluster-autoscaler/enabled,
k8s.io/cluster-autoscaler/my-demo-cluster
VERY IMPORTANT
Autoscaler finds ASGs using tags:
Required ASG tags:
k8s.io/cluster-autoscaler/enabled = true
k8s.io/cluster-autoscaler/my-demo-cluster = owned
If missing → autoscaler won’t work ❌
--expander=least-waste
Chooses best node group:
Minimizes unused resources
--skip-nodes-with-local-storage=false
Allows scaling down even if local storage exists

5. How your example should behave
Scenario 1: Current config
requests:
  cpu: 1
replicas: 2
Total = 2 CPU
If node has 4 CPU → no scaling
Scenario 2: Increase requests
requests:
  cpu: 3
replicas: 2
Total = 6 CPU
If node has 4 CPU:
1 pod fits
1 pod Pending → Autoscaler triggers ✔

6. Why nodes are not coming (root cause summary)
Most common reasons:
1. Requests too small
Autoscaler never triggers
2. ASG tags missing
Autoscaler cannot discover node group
3. IAM permission issue
Autoscaler cannot scale ASG
4. No Pending pods
Autoscaler only reacts to Pending pods
5. Max size reached
ASG already at max capacity

7. How to verify (VERY IMPORTANT)
Check pending pods
kubectl get pods
Look for:
Pending
Check autoscaler logs
kubectl logs -n kube-system deployment/cluster-autoscaler
Check nodes
kubectl get nodes -w
Check ASG
In AWS console:
Min / Max / Desired

8. Real-world mental model
Requests = trigger
Autoscaler = decision maker
ASG = executor
Final summary
Autoscaler works ONLY when:
Pod is Pending
Due to insufficient resources
Important:
Requests → triggers autoscaling
Limits → do NOT trigger autoscaling
